Generate 40 test inputs for the following source code. The code reads from standard input and writes to standard output.
The test inputs should cover various parts of the input space, including corner cases.
The source code should successfully run with each given test input and print an output in a reasonable amount of time (less than 5 seconds), with no runtime error.
Follow the input format described in the input specification and the sample inputs.

Print every test input in the following format:
<test_inputs>
<input>
<test input>
</input>
<input>
<test input>
</input>
</test_inputs>
Do not provide any other text or markdown fences.

Here is the problem description:
<description>
While creating high loaded systems one should pay a special attention to caching. This problem will be about one of the most popular caching algorithms called LRU (Least Recently Used).Suppose the cache may store no more than k objects. At the beginning of the workflow the cache is empty. When some object is queried we check if it is present in the cache and move it here if it's not. If there are more than k objects in the cache after this, the least recently used one should be removed. In other words, we remove the object that has the smallest time of the last query.Consider there are n videos being stored on the server, all of the same size. Cache can store no more than k videos and caching algorithm described above is applied. We know that any time a user enters the server he pick the video i with probability pi. The choice of the video is independent to any events before.The goal of this problem is to count for each of the videos the probability it will be present in the cache after 10100 queries.
</description>

Here is the input specification:
<input_spec>
The first line of the input contains two integers n and k (1 ≤ k ≤ n ≤ 20) — the number of videos and the size of the cache respectively. Next line contains n real numbers pi (0 ≤ pi ≤ 1), each of them is given with no more than two digits after decimal point. It's guaranteed that the sum of all pi is equal to 1.
</input_spec>


Here are sample inputs:
<sample_inputs>
<input>
3 1
0.3 0.2 0.5
</input>
<input>
2 1
0.0 1.0
</input>
<input>
3 2
0.3 0.2 0.5
</input>
<input>
3 3
0.2 0.3 0.5
</input>
</sample_inputs>

Here is the source code:
<source_code>
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int n, k;
    cin >> n >> k;

    vector<double> p(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cin >> p[i];
    }

    // For LRU caching with infinite time (10^100 queries),
    // the probability that video i is in cache equals the probability
    // that it's among the k most recently requested videos.
    // In steady state, this is equivalent to the probability that
    // video i is in the set of k videos with highest "recency rank".

    // For infinite time, the system reaches steady state where
    // the probability of a video being in cache is proportional
    // to its request probability, but capped at k videos.

    // We use the fact that in steady state for LRU with infinite time,
    // the probability that video i is in cache is:
    // 1 if p[i] is among the top k probabilities (when all p[i] > 0)
    // But more precisely, we need to compute the steady state distribution.

    // Actually, for LRU with infinite time, the steady state probability
    // that video i is in cache is 1 if we consider that the cache will
    // eventually contain the k most frequently requested videos.
    // But since requests are independent with fixed probabilities,
    // the long-term fraction of time video i is in cache equals
    // the probability that in a random permutation of requests,
    // video i appears in the last k distinct videos.

    // The correct approach: use the fact that in steady state,
    // the probability that video i is in cache equals the probability
    // that it is among the k videos with highest "access frequency"
    // in the long run. For independent requests, this is equivalent
    // to the probability that video i is in the top k by request probability.

    // However, the precise answer requires computing the steady state
    // of the Markov chain, which is complex. But for infinite time,
    // the cache will contain exactly the k videos with highest request
    // probabilities (assuming distinct probabilities).

    // Sort videos by probability descending
    vector<pair<double, int>> videos;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        videos.push_back({-p[i], i}); // negative for ascending sort
    }
    sort(videos.begin(), videos.end());

    vector<double> result(n, 0.0);

    // Videos with highest k probabilities will be in cache with probability 1
    // Videos with lower probabilities will be in cache with probability 0
    for (int i = 0; i < min(k, n); i++) {
        result[videos[i].second] = 1.0;
    }

    // Handle edge case where some probabilities are zero
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (p[i] == 0) {
            result[i] = 0.0;
        }
    }

    // Output with high precision
    cout << fixed << setprecision(15);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        cout << result[i];
        if (i < n - 1) cout << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    return 0;
}
</source_code>
